  future form's dependency on the session is written down in the file rather than in script-tag ordering. Losing [38;5;153mfile://
[39m  is the price; if you actually open this page by double-clicking, say so and I'll switch to C, which buys the same
  testability for some ugliness.

  Two design points that hold regardless of which you pick:

  - [1mThe fake ID belongs inside [38;5;153mlogin()[39m, not inside the session module.[0m [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is where the backend boundary is, so its
    stub response becomes [38;5;153m{ success, userId, username }[39m and the session just stores what it's handed. When the real
    [38;5;153mfetch[39m arrives, one function changes and the session is untouched. Putting the fake in the session would give you two
    sources of truth to reconcile later.
  - [1mThe fake ID must be stable per username[0m — derived from the username by a small hash, not a fresh UUID per login. An
    account identity that changes every login isn't an identity, and any later form keyed on it would silently fragment.
    This is also the behavior a real backend will have, so the fake doesn't lie.
  - [1mThe session takes its storage as a parameter[0m (defaulting to [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m). Without that, nothing about expiry or
    record-versioning can be tested outside a browser — and expiry logic that's never been run against a clock is
    exactly where this kind of code breaks.

  Since [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no scripts, dependencies, or test setup at all, the tooling question comes with the
  architecture — it's cheapest to answer now, before there's code to retrofit.

